WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.120
Good morning Hank, it's Sunday.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:05.260
Today I'm going to tell you five reasons why I think the presidential election in Iran was fraudulent.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.640
But first, we have to have an election of our own.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.040
Nerdfighters, the beard: Should I keep it or shave it?

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.920
Vote in comments. I realize this isn't an important election, but unlike Iran,

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.120
I will at least adhere to the results.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:23.120
[Music]

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.080
Okay Hank, so first, two sentences of background on Iran.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.820
Iran is an "Islamic Republic", which means that while the position of president is important,

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.900
it's not that important because ultimately, Iran's domestic and international affairs

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:41.420
are always decided by a council of religious leaders, which is led by the Ayatollah Khomeini.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:46.240
Every time Iran has a presidential election, like 37,000 moderates try to run for president,

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.900
but they are barred from doing so.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:52.320
Which has historically often led to low voter turnout because people don't show up to vote

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.620
for guys they don't like.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.520
Okay Hank, so a couple days ago, Iran had a presidential election and as you may know,

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:01.100
the incumbent president of Iran is a guy named Mahmoud Ahmadinejad,

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.980
who, to use a somewhat technical political science term, is a bit of a douche-nozzle.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.720
And in this election, there were 37,000 moderates who weren't allowed to run just like usual,

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.560
but there were also two quasi-reformist candidates

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.520
including a former prime minister of Iran named Mir-Hossein Mousavi.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:20.780
So on election day, a stunning 85% of eligible voters voted, and a couple hours after the polls closed

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.780
the interior ministry announced that Ahmadinejad was winning with 63% of the vote.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.780
Which Hank, as least as far as I'm concerned, is just the little, tiniest bit impossible.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:35.420
I maintain that Ahmadinejad, and possibly supreme leader Ayatollah Khomeini, stole the election.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.320
[Sniffs]

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.080
Is that a fatwa I smell on me?

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.860
Hank, I'm not saying that this election was invalid because a douche-nozzle happened to win it.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.780
Douche-nozzles win elections all the time, all over the world.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.420
What I'm saying is that there's just a lot of things that don't add up,

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.120
like #1: Tabriz.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.060
The reformist candidate Mousavi is from a city called Tabriz,

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:58.820
and historically, the city of Tabriz always votes for Mr. Mousavi or whoever the guy is from his ethnic group

00:01:58.820 --> 00:01:59.480
who's running.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:04.420
And yet, inexplicably, in this election, they apparently voted 2 to 1 for Ahmadinejad.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:07.460
I mean, Hank, its the equivalent of John McCain winning Chicago.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.580
Reason two: 2005.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:12.700
Hank, the last time Iran had a presidential election was in 2005,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.100
and even though a ton of people didn't show up to the polls because they were mad about their

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.800
candidates not being on the ballot,

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:22.760
Ahmadinejad, who was less crazy and more popular than he is now,

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.160
still wasn't able to avoid a runoff.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.780
Which leads me to reason 3: Turnout.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.280
The turnout in this election was ridiculously high by Iranian standards.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.880
And in the last twenty years, every time there has been high turnout, a reformist candidate has won.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:42.060
All of the pre-election polls indicated that the only chance Ahmadinejad had

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.360
was if the turnout was extremely low.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:46.220
Number 4: Superhuman counting.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:50.560
Hank, despite the incredibly high turnout, the interior ministry managed to announce the election results

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.820
within, like, two hours of polls closing.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:58.440
Hank, they counted like ten million more ballots thirty times faster than they ever have in the past.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.760
It's almost like they weren't counting them at all.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.460
And number 5: The possible acknowledgment.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.480
Hank, because the Iranian government is starting to kick out foreign journalists

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.840
and shut down websites, it's a little hard to get reliable information, even for people like me

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:13.400
who have spent the entire day reading Iranian Twitters.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.380
Link in the sidebar.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:19.460
But many people from inside Mousavi's campaign are reporting that the interior ministry called him

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.780
to say that he had won the election,

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:26.800
shortly before that same interior ministry went on TV and announced that Mousavi had, in fact,

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:28.000
lost the election.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.220
Hank, we still don't know the whole story about the elections. It's possible that somehow they weren't rigged.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.320
But I do hope that the government of Iran has the decency to honor the will of its people.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:41.140
As it says in the Quran, "Truly, God does not guide those who transgress and lie."

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.460
Hank, like I said, I have links in the sidebar to the Twitters of young Iranians

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.060
who are tweeting though all of this.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.580
You often hear that nothing interesting can be said in 140 characters.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:53.060
Obviously the people who say that aren't following the young Iranian woman who just wrote:

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:56.160
"If Iran sleeps tonight, it will sleep forever."

